  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` rejected prices with thousands separators, such as `\"1,299.00\"`. Its regex only allowed plain digits, so `match()` returned `null`. The next line then read `m[1]` and threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`, which `server.js` turned into a 500.\n\nThe log supports this. All 37 `POST /checkout 500` errors have the same TypeError. Every one has a payload price containing a comma (`1,299.00`, `2,450.50` or `1,005.99`). Every price with a comma failed, and none of the plain prices did. That's why the 500s looked intermittent: they only hit orders for items priced at 1,000 or more.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- `parseAmount` now accepts properly grouped thousands (`1,299.00`, `1,234,567`) and strips the commas.\n- Input that isn't a valid amount now throws `Error('Invalid amount: <input>')`, as the function's own comment promised, instead of an accidental TypeError. A malformed price like `1,29.00` still makes checkout return a 500. The error is now explicit, but I didn't change `server.js` to return a 4xx for bad input.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):** I added regression tests for comma-grouped amounts, checkout with a `1,299.00` price, and invalid-input errors. `npm test` passes with all 7 tests. I didn't run the new tests against the old code.",
